+# Review: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockShop.server.luau`
+
+Findings are ordered by impact. The first group lets a player cheat or lose
+data, so fix those before building more on this script. Line numbers refer to
+the file as it is at commit `5b54477`.
+
+## Checks run
+
+- `selene` and `stylua --check` both pass on this file.
+- `luau-lsp analyze` reports `ImplicitReturn` on `getOwnerName` (line 56). That
+  warning is a real bug; see finding 2.
+- `luau-lsp` also reports both requires as "Unknown require". That is because
+  `sourcemap.json` is out of date: it has no entry for `BlockShop`,
+  `BlockTypes`, or `PlacementRules`. Run `rojo sourcemap` again before relying
+  on type-check results. I did not regenerate it, so I have no strict type
+  check for this file.
+
+---
+
+## Critical: cheating and data loss
+
+### 1. Remote arguments are trusted without checks
+
+**Where:** `buyRemote` handler (lines 84-103), `placeRemote` handler (lines
+105-125), `removeRemote` handler (lines 127-140).
+
+**What's wrong:** The annotations `bt: string`, `amt: number`, `pos: Vector3`,
+and `part: Instance` tell the type checker to trust whatever the client sends.
+An exploiter can send any value:
+
+- **`amt = 0/0` (NaN)** gets past `amt < 1 or amt > MAX_PURCHASE`, because
+  every comparison with NaN is false. `price` becomes NaN, `coins < price` is
+  false, and `setCoins` is called with NaN. Depending on what the DataStore
+  does with NaN, either the call throws or the saved balance is ruined. If NaN
+  is saved, every later `coins < price` check is false, so all purchases
+  become free from then on.
+- **A fractional `amt`** such as `1.5` is accepted. The player gets a fraction
+  of a block in stock that can never be placed.
+- **A non-number `amt` or an unknown `bt`** throws an error inside the handler
+  (`PRICES[bt] * amt` is nil arithmetic). That only kills that call, but it
+  floods the server output.
+- **A NaN `pos`** gets past every check in `PlacementRules.check`: the height
+  and reach comparisons are false, and the overlap query finds nothing. The
+  block is created at a NaN position, so the reach limit does not apply.
+- **`part = nil`** or a non-Instance value makes `part.Parent` throw.
+
+**What to do instead:** Type every argument after the player as `unknown`, and
+narrow it before using it. Return early when a check fails:
+
+```lua
+buyRemote.OnServerEvent:Connect(function(player: Player, blockType: unknown, amount: unknown)
+	if not BlockTypes.isBlockType(blockType) then
+		return
+	end
+	if
+		typeof(amount) ~= "number"
+		or amount ~= math.floor(amount) -- also rejects NaN
+		or amount < 1
+		or amount > MAX_PURCHASE
+	then
+		return
+	end
+	-- blockType :: BlockTypes.BlockType, amount: number from here on
+end)
+```
+
+For `pos`, check `typeof(pos) == "Vector3"` and reject non-finite components
+(`pos.X == pos.X` and `math.abs(pos.X) ~= math.huge`, for each axis). For
+`part`, check `typeof(part) == "Instance"` and `part:IsA("BasePart")` before
+reading `Parent`.
+
+### 2. Removing a block whose owner has left always errors
+
+**Where:** `getOwnerName` (lines 50-57) and the check in `removeRemote` (lines
+132-136).
+
+**What's wrong:** When no player in the server matches `OwnerId`,
+`getOwnerName` reaches its end without a `return` and returns no values at
+all, not `nil`. `tostring()` with no argument throws "missing argument #1", so
+line 133 throws in the exact case the comment says it handles. Players can
+never clear away a block whose owner has left.
+
+The `"nil"` string sentinel is also fragile, and it compares names when a
+stable `UserId` is available.
+
+**What to do instead:** Compare user IDs, and ask `Players` directly whether
+the owner is still in the server:
+
+```lua
+local ownerId = part:GetAttribute("OwnerId")
+local isOwnerPresent = typeof(ownerId) == "number"
+	and Players:GetPlayerByUserId(ownerId) ~= nil
+if isOwnerPresent and ownerId ~= player.UserId then
+	return
+end
+```
+
+That makes `getOwnerName` unnecessary. If you keep a helper like it, give it a
+return type (`: string?`) and end it with `return nil`. Fix finding 3 at the
+same time, because once this check works, finding 3 can be exploited.
+
+### 3. Clearing someone else's block puts it in your stock
+
+**Where:** line 139, together with the rule in the comment on line 132.
+
+**What's wrong:** After finding 2 is fixed, anyone can remove a block whose
+owner has left, and line 139 adds that block to the remover's stock. A player
+can buy blocks on one account, place them, leave, and collect them on a second
+account. Any player can also farm whatever a departed player built.
+
+**What to do instead:** Refund only the owner. Check
+`ownerId == player.UserId` before line 139. Clearing an abandoned block just
+destroys it. You could also destroy a player's blocks in `PlayerRemoving` (or
+after a grace period), so abandoned blocks never pile up.
+
+### 4. Concurrent purchases can spend the same coins twice
+
+**Where:** `getCoins` / `setCoins` (lines 42-48), called from the buy handler
+(lines 90-94).
+
+**What's wrong:** `GetAsync` yields. If a client fires `BuyBlocks` twice in a
+row, both handlers read the same balance before either one writes. Each then
+writes `coins - price`, so the player pays once and gets stock twice. On top
+of that:
+
+- Every purchase makes a DataStore read and a DataStore write. Spamming the
+  remote uses up the server's request budget, and later calls fail, including
+  calls made for other players.
+- Neither call is wrapped in `pcall`. A DataStore outage or throttle error
+  throws inside the handler, and the purchase fails silently.
+- `SetAsync` blindly overwrites. Two servers holding the same player (during a
+  teleport or rejoin) can overwrite each other's balance.
+
+**What to do instead:** Load the balance once in `PlayerAdded` into an
+in-memory table, and spend from that table without yielding, so the check and
+the deduction happen atomically. Save with `UpdateAsync` inside `pcall`, on
+`PlayerRemoving`, on a periodic autosave, and in `game:BindToClose`. If the
+game will have more saved data than coins, use a session-locking profile
+library (such as ProfileStore) instead of writing this yourself. Name any
+helper that yields with an `Async` suffix (`loadCoinsAsync`).
+
+### 5. Bought blocks are lost when the player leaves
+
+**Where:** `stock` (line 40) and `PlayerRemoving` (lines 146-148).
+
+**What's wrong:** Coins are saved to the DataStore, but the stock they paid for
+lives only in memory and is cleared on leave. A player who buys 50 blocks and
+leaves before placing them loses the blocks and keeps paying for them.
+
+**What to do instead:** Save stock in the same record as coins, and write both
+together (see finding 4). Then one write cannot succeed while the other fails.
+
+---
+
+## High: bugs in normal play
+
+### 6. `ShowReceipts = false` is ignored
+
+**Where:** line 99.
+
+**What's wrong:** `plr:GetAttribute("ShowReceipts") or DEFAULT_SHOW_RECEIPTS`
+replaces `false` with `true`, because `or` treats `false` the same as `nil`.
+Turning receipts off has no effect. `AutoAnchor` on lines 118-121 handles this
+correctly.
+
+**What to do instead:** Use the same `== nil` pattern as `AutoAnchor`. If more
+per-player settings are coming, use a small helper such as
+`getSetting(player, name, default)`.
+
+### 7. Players who join before the handler connects have no stock table
+
+**Where:** `Players.PlayerAdded` (lines 142-144), and every `stock[plr]` read
+(lines 96, 106, 139).
+
+**What's wrong:** `PlayerAdded` only fires for players who join after it is
+connected. In a Studio play test, or if anything above line 142 ever yields
+(the requires, or a future DataStore load), a player can already be in the
+server. That player never gets a table, and every remote they fire throws on
+`stock[plr][...]`. The same thing happens for a remote that arrives after
+`PlayerRemoving` has cleared the entry.
+
+**What to do instead:** After connecting `PlayerAdded`, run the same setup
+function for each player in `Players:GetPlayers()`. In each handler, return
+early when `stock[player]` is nil. Type the table as
+`{ [Player]: { [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number? }? }`, so the checker requires
+those nil checks.
+
+---
+
+## Medium: design that will slow you down
+
+### 8. Prices are kept apart from the block type list
+
+**Where:** `PRICES` (lines 14-20).
+
+**What's wrong:** `BlockTypes` already owns the list of block types. `PRICES`
+repeats the list as plain `string` keys. If you add a block type to
+`BlockTypes` and forget a price, every purchase of that type throws on nil
+arithmetic, and the type checker cannot warn you.
+
+**What to do instead:** Add `Price` to `BlockStyle` in `BlockTypes` (or add a
+`BlockTypes.Prices` table typed `{ [BlockType]: number }`). Then each type's
+data lives in one place, and the client shop UI can read the same prices.
+
+### 9. Remotes are created at runtime, loose in ReplicatedStorage
+
+**Where:** `makeRemote` and the four calls to it (lines 24-34).
+
+**What's wrong:** The client cannot know that the remotes exist until this
+script has run, so every client script must use `WaitForChild`. Nothing stops
+another server script from making a second `BuyBlocks`. Four loose instances
+in the root of ReplicatedStorage also get harder to manage as more systems add
+theirs.
+
+**What to do instead:** Declare the remotes in the project, either as
+`.model.json` files or as entries in `default.project.json` under a
+`ReplicatedStorage.Remotes` folder. Then both sides reference them directly.
+If you'd rather keep creating them in code, put them in one shared module that
+both sides require, inside a `Remotes` folder.
+
+### 10. Clients can flood the server output
+
+**Where:** `warn` on line 114, `print` on line 79, and the errors from
+finding 1.
+
+**What's wrong:** Every placement attempt prints or warns. A client firing the
+remote in a loop fills the output and makes real errors hard to find. The
+failure reason is also never sent back to the client, so the player cannot see
+why the block did not appear.
+
+**What to do instead:** Drop the server-side logging, or keep it behind a
+debug flag. Return the reason to the client: send it on a remote, or switch
+`PlaceBlock` to a `RemoteFunction`. The client already runs
+`PlacementRules.check` for its preview, so it can usually show the reason
+before sending anything. Consider a simple per-player rate limit on all three
+remotes.
+
+### 11. `placeBlock` takes two boolean flags
+
+**Where:** `placeBlock` signature (lines 59-65), call on line 124.
+
+**What's wrong:** `placeBlock(plr, center, bt, autoAnchor, true)` cannot be
+read without opening the function. The `log` flag is `true` at the only call
+site, so it does nothing.
+
+**What to do instead:** Remove `log` (see finding 10). Pass anchoring in an
+options table (`{ Anchored = isAutoAnchored }`), or read the player's setting
+inside `placeBlock`. Type `bt` as `BlockTypes.BlockType`, so the cast on line
+66 is no longer needed: the handler narrows the value once (finding 1).
+
+---
+
+## Low: style and consistency
+
+### 12. Missing `--!strict`
+
+**Where:** line 1.
+
+`BlockTypes` and `PlacementRules` both run in strict mode, but this script
+does not, so most of the type problems above go unreported. Add `--!strict` as
+the first line. Expect new warnings, which mostly match findings 1, 2, and 7.
+
+### 13. Names
+
+**Where:** throughout.
+
+- Spell out abbreviations: `plr` to `player`, `amt` to `amount`, `bt` to
+  `blockType`, `p` to `otherPlayer`, `pos` to `position`, `ok` to
+  `canPlace`.
+- Name functions that yield with `Async` (`getCoins`, `setCoins`; see finding
+  4).
+- Name the remote handlers for when they run (`onBuyBlocks`, `onPlaceBlock`,
+  `onRemoveBlock`, `onPlayerAdded`, `onPlayerRemoving`), and connect them by
+  name. Then the bottom of the file shows how the script is wired together.
+- Name booleans as yes/no questions: `autoAnchor` to `isAutoAnchored`,
+  `showReceipts` to `shouldShowReceipts`.
+
+### 14. Return type on `getOwnerName`
+
+**Where:** line 50.
+
+If the function stays, give it `: string?` and an explicit `return nil` at the
+end. Finding 2 removes the need for it.
+
+---
+
+## Suggested order of work
+
+1. Validate remote arguments (1) and fix the owner check and refund (2, 3)
+   together.
+2. Move coins and stock to an in-memory session record that is loaded on join
+   and saved with `UpdateAsync` (4, 5). Set up existing players at startup at
+   the same time (7).
+3. Fix the receipt setting (6), then the design items (8-11) as you build on
+   the shop.
+4. Add `--!strict` and rename while making the changes above (12-14), so the
+   style changes come along with real changes instead of making a separate
+   diff.
